## Priority 1. Increase uptake and targeting of seasonal vaccination

**Why it matters:** Influenza, COVID-19 and pneumococcal disease contribute to winter consultations, hospital admissions, excess mortality and workforce absence. Uptake is often lower in deprived communities, younger high-risk adults, some ethnic minority groups and people experiencing homelessness.

| Intervention | Level and mechanism | Resources and implementation requirements | Evidence, feasibility and barriers | Suggested indicators |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **1.1 Proactive, risk-stratified vaccination call–recall** | **Clinical/organisational.** Use practice registers to invite people with chronic respiratory disease, cardiovascular disease, diabetes, immunosuppression, pregnancy and frailty. Reduces missed opportunities. | **Low–medium:** accurate registers, SMS/letter/telephone capacity and protected vaccination sessions. Requires inclusion of people who do not routinely attend. | **Strong evidence** that organised reminders and recall improve vaccination uptake, although the size of benefit varies. Feasible within 3 years. Barriers include inaccurate registers, language barriers and staff capacity. | Uptake by eligible group; uptake by deprivation, ethnicity and age; missed-opportunity rate; vaccination before peak winter demand. |
| **1.2 Opportunistic vaccination in non-traditional settings** | **Organisational/policy.** Offer vaccination in pharmacies, care homes, workplaces, homeless-health services, community centres and during other NHS contacts. Reduces access barriers. | **Medium:** trained vaccinators, supply, data-sharing and remuneration. Requires interoperable recording. | **Strong-to-moderate evidence** for pharmacy and community vaccination improving access; equity effects are context-dependent. Usually feasible within 3 years. | Uptake in underserved groups; proportion vaccinated outside general practice; travel distance; failed appointment rate. |
| **1.3 Targeted culturally and linguistically adapted communications** | **Organisational/community.** Uses trusted messengers and tailored materials to address concerns and practical barriers. | **Low–medium:** co-design with communities, interpreters and local communication channels. | **Promising/undervalued.** Evidence supports tailored reminders, but effects on sustained uptake are heterogeneous. Feasible within 3 years. | Uptake gap between least and most deprived groups; patient-reported trust and accessibility; no-show rates. |

**Context sensitivity:** High in areas with low trust, limited pharmacy capacity or poor digital access.

**Relevant policy sources:** NHS England seasonal vaccination programme guidance; UK Health Security Agency (UKHSA) surveillance and immunisation guidance.

