## Priority 2. Provide structured ARI self-care, triage and safety-netting

**Why it matters:** Many winter consultations concern self-limiting coughs, sore throats, viral upper-respiratory infections and uncomplicated fever. The challenge is not simply reducing contacts; it is helping patients obtain appropriate care without delaying treatment for serious disease.

| Intervention | Level and mechanism | Resources and implementation requirements | Evidence, feasibility and barriers | Suggested indicators |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **2.1 Standardised ARI assessment and safety-netting template** | **Clinical.** A structured template records duration, severity, vital signs where appropriate, risk factors, red flags, working diagnosis, expected course and explicit reconsultation instructions. Reduces omission and unwarranted variation. | **Low:** template integrated into the electronic record, clinician training and periodic audit. | **Strong evidence for components of safety-netting and structured assessment**, but direct evidence for a single electronic template reducing admissions is limited. Feasible within 3 years. | Documentation of red flags and safety-netting; reattendance within 7 and 14 days; delayed diagnoses; antibiotic prescribing. |
| **2.2 Public-facing, accessible self-care information** | **Clinical/organisational.** Provides realistic illness-duration advice, symptom-control guidance and clear escalation criteria. Reduces anxiety-driven consultations. | **Low:** NHS-approved materials, translations, easy-read and non-digital alternatives. | **Moderate evidence** that communication and delayed-prescribing strategies reduce antibiotic use and improve understanding. Feasible within 3 years. | ARI contacts per 1,000 population; patient knowledge of expected duration; reattendance; complaints relating to access or dismissal. |
| **2.3 Pharmacist-supported minor-illness pathway** | **Organisational.** Community pharmacists assess eligible low-risk presentations, provide symptomatic advice and refer red-flag cases. | **Medium:** local protocols, access to clinical records where lawful, referral routes and remuneration. | **Moderate evidence** for pharmacy-based minor-ailment schemes, but NHS-wide impact depends on referral quality and capacity. Feasible within 3–5 years. | Pharmacy consultations; appropriate referrals; antibiotic supply; emergency referrals; patient-reported experience. |

**Medico-legal requirement:** Self-care material must not be used as a substitute for clinical assessment where red flags, significant comorbidity, safeguarding concerns or diagnostic uncertainty exist.

