## Priority 3. Reduce inappropriate antibiotic prescribing while maintaining access to treatment

**Why it matters:** Winter ARI demand creates pressure for rapid prescribing. Unnecessary antibiotics cause adverse effects, contribute to antimicrobial resistance and may reinforce expectations of antibiotics for viral illness.

| Intervention | Level and mechanism | Resources and implementation requirements | Evidence, feasibility and barriers | Suggested indicators |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **3.1 Delayed antibiotic prescriptions for selected low-risk infections** | **Clinical.** A prescription is used only if symptoms fail to improve or worsen after an agreed period. Preserves access while reducing immediate antibiotic use. | **Low:** clinician training, patient instructions and consistent coding. | **Strong evidence** from randomised trials and systematic reviews that delayed prescribing substantially reduces antibiotic use without worsening most clinical outcomes in suitable patients. Feasible within 3 years. | Antibiotic prescribing by diagnosis; delayed-prescription proportion; complications; reconsultation; patient satisfaction. |
| **3.2 Point-of-care C-reactive protein testing for selected acute cough presentations** | **Clinical/organisational.** Supports differentiation of likely bacterial lower-respiratory infection from self-limiting illness and reduces diagnostic uncertainty. | **Medium–high:** equipment, quality control, consumables, staff training and interpretation protocols. | **Moderate-to-strong evidence** for reduced antibiotic prescribing in selected acute respiratory presentations, with variable effects by setting and baseline prescribing. Feasible within 3–5 years where infrastructure permits. | Antibiotic prescribing; CRP test rate; safety-netting; follow-up admissions; cost per avoided prescription. |
| **3.3 Audit-and-feedback with peer comparison and clinician communication training** | **Organisational.** Gives clinicians timely data against agreed standards and supports better explanations of non-antibiotic management. | **Low–medium:** prescribing dashboards, protected review time and trained facilitators. | **Strong evidence** that audit-feedback and communication interventions can reduce prescribing, although effects are usually modest and may attenuate. Feasible within 3 years. | Prescribing rate and variation; broad-spectrum prescribing; prescribing by clinician and deprivation; patient-reported involvement. |

**Important caution:** Targets must not encourage under-prescribing in older people, immunocompromised patients or those with suspected pneumonia, sepsis or significant comorbidity.

