## Priority 4. Redesign same-day urgent care around integrated neighbourhood pathways

**Why it matters:** Fragmented access routes generate duplicated assessments, repeated telephone calls, avoidable ambulance use and emergency-department attendance.

| Intervention | Level and mechanism | Resources and implementation requirements | Evidence, feasibility and barriers | Suggested indicators |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **4.1 Single clinical navigation process for urgent contacts** | **Organisational.** Trained navigators or clinicians direct patients to general practice, pharmacy, community nursing, urgent treatment centres, ambulance services or self-care according to need. | **Medium:** trained staff, local directory of services, clinical escalation and shared protocols. | **Moderate evidence**, mainly from evaluations of telephone triage and urgent-care redesign; outcomes depend heavily on implementation. Feasible within 3–5 years. | Time to clinically appropriate assessment; transfers between services; avoidable ED attendances; safety incidents. |
| **4.2 Same-day multidisciplinary neighbourhood respiratory/frailty response** | **Organisational.** General practice, community nursing, paramedics, therapists and geriatric expertise assess patients at home or in community hubs. | **High:** workforce, vehicles, diagnostics, shared records and seven-day arrangements. | **Promising but context-dependent.** Evidence for admission avoidance in selected frail and respiratory patients is supportive but heterogeneous. Usually 3–5 years. | Admission rate; ambulance conveyance; response time; patient-reported ability to remain at home; staff workload. |
| **4.3 Direct referral from general practice to community diagnostics and specialist advice** | **Policy/organisational.** Rapid access to chest radiography, blood tests, oxygen assessment or specialist advice reduces emergency referrals caused by diagnostic uncertainty. | **Medium–high:** diagnostic capacity, referral criteria and consultant time. | **Promising/undervalued.** Strong face validity, but NHS-wide cost-effectiveness and safety evidence are limited. | ED referrals; time to test; diagnostic turnaround; missed serious disease; clinician confidence. |

**Resource-constrained settings:** A full multidisciplinary team is difficult to reproduce. A lower-resource version can use agreed telephone advice, community nursing escalation and a single referral directory.

