## Priority 5. Expand safe hospital-at-home and virtual ward care for suitable patients

**Why it matters:** Hospital beds become scarce during winter. Some patients with COPD exacerbations, heart failure, respiratory infection or frailty can be treated at home, but remote care must not become a mechanism for shifting work and risk to families.

| Intervention | Level and mechanism | Resources and implementation requirements | Evidence, feasibility and barriers | Suggested indicators |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **5.1 Clinically governed hospital-at-home pathways** | **Organisational.** Provides hospital-level assessment, treatment and review at home for carefully selected patients. Releases beds and may improve person-centredness. | **High:** visiting staff, diagnostics, medicines, rapid escalation and 24-hour clinical governance. | **Strong evidence in selected conditions** from hospital-at-home reviews, but generalisability to all winter ARIs is limited. 3–5 years. | Admissions avoided; 30-day readmission; mortality; patient experience; carer burden; adverse events. |
| **5.2 Virtual wards using remote monitoring only where clinically necessary** | **Organisational/clinical.** Supports review of physiological observations and symptoms, with defined thresholds for escalation. | **Medium–high:** devices, connectivity, staff reviewing data and clear response protocols. | **Promising, with mixed evidence.** Benefits are highly dependent on patient selection and staffing; technology alone is not an intervention. 3–5 years. | Escalation response time; false alerts; admission and readmission; inequalities in uptake; staff time per patient. |
| **5.3 Home pulse-oximetry with structured escalation protocols** | **Clinical/organisational.** Adds objective monitoring for selected patients with respiratory illness. | **Low–medium:** validated devices, instruction, interpretation and telephone support. | **Moderate evidence**, particularly from COVID-19-era pathways; applicability to other ARIs and darker skin tones requires careful validation and governance. 1–3 years. | Device-related safety incidents; escalation accuracy; admissions; patient understanding; performance by ethnicity and device type. |

**Highly context-sensitive:** Requires reliable community staffing. Remote monitoring without timely clinical response may increase rather than reduce risk.

