## Priority 6. Improve hospital flow, discharge and readmission prevention

**Why it matters:** Winter pressures are amplified when medically fit patients remain in hospital, when discharge services are delayed or when patients return because medicines, follow-up or community support are inadequate.

| Intervention | Level and mechanism | Resources and implementation requirements | Evidence, feasibility and barriers | Suggested indicators |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **6.1 Multidisciplinary discharge planning from admission** | **Clinical/organisational.** Identifies discharge needs early and coordinates medicines, oxygen, equipment, follow-up and social support. | **Medium:** multidisciplinary meetings, discharge coordinators and community capacity. | **Moderate evidence** that structured discharge planning can reduce length of stay and readmission in some populations, though effects are variable. 1–3 years. | Length of stay; delayed transfers; 7- and 30-day readmission; discharge before midday; patient understanding. |
| **6.2 Seven-day medicines reconciliation and post-discharge telephone review** | **Clinical/organisational.** Prevents medication discrepancies, missed follow-up and early deterioration. | **Medium:** pharmacist and nursing time, shared records and escalation routes. | **Moderate evidence** for medicines reconciliation and transitional-care components; not every programme reduces readmissions. 1–3 years. | Medication discrepancies; post-discharge contacts; readmission; adverse drug events; completion of follow-up. |
| **6.3 Frailty-focused discharge-to-assess and intermediate care** | **Organisational/policy.** Assesses function in the person’s usual environment and provides rehabilitation or reablement rather than prolonged inpatient care. | **High:** social care, therapy and community nursing capacity. | **Moderate but heterogeneous evidence.** Strongly dependent on social-care availability. 3–5 years. | Delayed-discharge days; care-home admission; functional status; readmission; carer experience. |

**Equity risk:** If community services are weaker in deprived or rural areas, discharge-at-home programmes may widen inequalities.

