## Priority 7. Strengthen care-home and domiciliary-care prevention and escalation

**Why it matters:** Care-home residents have high risk of influenza, COVID-19, pneumonia, dehydration, delirium and hospitalisation. Avoidable transfers are distressing, but not all hospital transfers are avoidable or inappropriate.

| Intervention | Level and mechanism | Resources and implementation requirements | Evidence, feasibility and barriers | Suggested indicators |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **7.1 Vaccination and outbreak-response support for residents and staff** | **Organisational/policy.** Improves population protection and enables early infection-control action. | **Medium:** outreach vaccinators, surveillance, PPE and infection-control expertise. | **Strong evidence for vaccination reducing severe respiratory infection**, but staff vaccination effects on resident outcomes are less certain. 1–3 years. | Resident and staff uptake; outbreak size; hospital transfers; mortality; time from suspected outbreak to response. |
| **7.2 Structured deteriorating-resident assessment and anticipatory care planning** | **Clinical/organisational.** Records treatment preferences, escalation plans and clinical triggers, enabling timely community treatment when appropriate. | **Medium:** clinician time, staff training and accessible records. | **Moderate evidence** for advance care planning and structured communication; effects on hospital use vary. 1–3 years. | Proportion with current care plan; concordance with preferences; avoidable transfers; complaints; documented review. |
| **7.3 Rapid community geriatrician, nurse or paramedic review** | **Organisational.** Provides assessment for delirium, dehydration, hypoxia, infection and falls before transfer decisions. | **Medium–high:** seven-day response team, diagnostics and prescribing authority. | **Promising/context-dependent**, supported mainly by observational studies and service evaluations. 3–5 years. | Response time; transfers avoided; adverse events; resident and family experience; staff confidence. |

