## Priority 8. Protect the health, safety and retention of the NHS workforce

**Why it matters:** Staff sickness, burnout, moral injury, violence and workload intensification directly reduce winter capacity and may compromise safety.

| Intervention | Level and mechanism | Resources and implementation requirements | Evidence, feasibility and barriers | Suggested indicators |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **8.1 Organisational workload redesign, not solely resilience training** | **Organisational.** Reduces unnecessary administrative work, improves role clarity, protects breaks and balances demand with staffing. | **Medium–high:** workforce planning, demand-capacity analysis and managerial action. | **Strong evidence** that organisation-directed interventions are more effective for burnout than individual-only approaches, although effects are modest. 1–3 years. | Sickness absence; vacancy and turnover; burnout; overtime; patient-safety incidents; work intensity. |
| **8.2 Protected team huddles and escalation arrangements during winter** | **Clinical/organisational.** Makes staffing gaps, deteriorating patients and capacity constraints visible early. | **Low–medium:** brief protected time and escalation policy. | **Moderate evidence** from patient-safety and team-communication literature; direct winter evidence is limited. 1–3 years. | Huddle completion; escalation response; near misses; staff psychological safety; unplanned overtime. |
| **8.3 Violence prevention and safe lone-working systems** | **Organisational/policy.** Risk assessment, staff training, chaperoning, security and incident follow-up reduce worker harm. | **Medium:** security infrastructure, training and reporting systems. | **Moderate evidence**, with substantial variation by setting. 1–3 years. | Violence incidents; staff injury; response time; reporting completeness; staff perception of safety. |

**Undervalued intervention:** Work redesign is often less visible than new technology but is more likely to produce sustainable capacity than asking staff to work faster.

