## Priority 9. Use interoperable digital decision support safely

**Why it matters:** Digital tools can support triage, prescribing, vaccination recalls and documentation, but poorly designed systems can increase workload, inequity, automation bias and medico-legal risk.

| Intervention | Level and mechanism | Resources and implementation requirements | Evidence, feasibility and barriers | Suggested indicators |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **9.1 Electronic ARI decision-support templates with override and safety-net prompts** | **Clinical/organisational.** Standardises history-taking, red-flag assessment, antibiotic review and follow-up. | **Low–medium:** co-designed software, clinical validation, usability testing and governance. | **Moderate evidence** for decision-support improving process measures; evidence for patient outcomes is less consistent. 1–3 years. | Completion of key fields; antibiotic prescribing; clinician time; override rates; safety incidents. |
| **9.2 Shared care record and real-time urgent-care information exchange** | **Policy/organisational.** Prevents repeated histories and allows pharmacists, GPs, community teams and hospitals to see relevant diagnoses, medicines and care plans. | **High:** interoperability, information governance, identity matching and workforce training. | **Promising but implementation-dependent.** Evidence supports better information continuity, but NHS-wide outcome effects are difficult to isolate. 3–5 years. | Record access; duplicate assessments; medication discrepancies; referral delays; information-related incidents. |
| **9.3 Digital-first access with a guaranteed non-digital route** | **Organisational.** Uses online forms, SMS and telephone routing for suitable patients while preserving face-to-face access for those needing it. | **Medium:** platform, triage staff and accessibility support. | **Mixed evidence.** Digital access may improve convenience but can increase inequity and workload if poorly designed. 1–3 years. | Time to care; abandonment; conversion to face-to-face; access by deprivation, disability, age and language; complaints. |

**High-risk area:** Artificial intelligence should initially be used as clinician-support only, with local validation, audit trails, human review, data protection assessment and clear accountability. It should not independently determine urgency, diagnosis or treatment in routine NHS primary care without robust evidence and regulation.

