## Priority 10. Embed environmental sustainability into winter respiratory pathways

**Why it matters:** Winter respiratory illness is affected by air pollution, housing quality, fuel poverty and climate-related extremes. Healthcare delivery also generates emissions through travel, medicines, inhalers, procurement and unnecessary admissions.

| Intervention | Level and mechanism | Resources and implementation requirements | Evidence, feasibility and barriers | Suggested indicators |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **10.1 Reduce unnecessary patient and staff travel through safe local care and remote follow-up** | **Organisational.** Prevents travel where remote review is clinically appropriate while retaining face-to-face access when needed. | **Low–medium:** reliable digital access, travel assessment and local services. | **Moderate evidence** that remote care can reduce travel, but total emissions may increase if digital infrastructure and duplicate contacts expand. 1–3 years. | Travel avoided; emissions estimate; clinical outcomes; digital-exclusion rate; repeat contacts. |
| **10.2 Respiratory inhaler optimisation and environmentally preferable prescribing where clinically equivalent** | **Clinical/policy.** Reduces overuse and selects lower-carbon options without compromising disease control. | **Low–medium:** prescribing review, formularies and patient education. | **Moderate evidence** for guideline-concordant inhaler optimisation; direct winter-pressure effects are indirect. 1–3 years. | Inhaler carbon estimates; exacerbations; oral steroid courses; patient technique and control. |
| **10.3 Link respiratory care with fuel-poverty, housing and air-quality support** | **Organisational/community/policy.** Addresses upstream triggers of respiratory exacerbations and inability to heat homes. | **Medium–high:** housing, local authority and voluntary-sector collaboration. | **Promising and undervalued.** Evidence for housing and fuel-poverty interventions improving respiratory outcomes is supportive but heterogeneous, and NHS attribution is difficult. 3–10 years. | Exacerbations; admissions; referrals completed; indoor temperature/fuel-security measures; health inequalities. |

**Context sensitivity:** This is highly dependent on local authority and housing-sector capacity, but it is globally relevant as a health-determinants intervention.

